# Test 5: Geometry-B (concentric hyper-shells, curved non-matching
# interface), p1 = p2 = 2, RL-RBF with radius factor r_f = 50.
# Shell refinement 6/5: master 12,681,474 DoFs (98,306 on the interface),
# slave 1,597,570 (24,578), as many DoFs as Test 4, so the two differ only
# in the degree and the radius. (The paper's original Test 5 was one level
# coarser, 1.8e6 DoFs in total: too few per process for strong scaling to
# 768 processes.)
# Radius r = r_f * h_avg of each subdomain's own mesh: 1.07 (master) and
# 2.97 (slave) against an interface diameter of 1.5. r_f = 10, the original
# choice, is in the stationary regime of RBF interpolation (support
# shrinking with h): H1 convergence of order 0.5 instead of 2; r_f = 50
# gives order 2.3 (results/scalability/radius_study/). Phi is then dense
# (master ~50%, slave 100%) and is factored once with ScaLAPACK.
subsection Geometry
  set Type                     = hyper_shells
  set Inner radius             = 0.5
  set Interface radius         = 0.75
  set Outer radius             = 1.0
  set Shell refinement master  = 6
  set Shell refinement slave   = 5
end

subsection Discretization
  set Master degree = 2
  set Slave degree  = 2
  set RBF radius        = 0.0
  set RBF radius factor = 50
end

subsection Boundary conditions
  set Dirichlet ids master = 0
  set Neumann ids master   =
  set Interface id master  = 1
  set Dirichlet ids slave = 1
  set Neumann ids slave   =
  set Interface id slave  = 0
end

subsection Solver
  set GMRES tolerance      = 1e-14
  set GMRES reduction      = 1e-8
  set GMRES max iterations = 1000
end
